{id:"BG001",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/0/text",fact:"For continuous L2-valued smooth-stage integrands on a compact step, the integral from the step start is differentiable with derivative equal to the integrand and value zero at the start. A finite concatenation of continuous piecewise smooth increments whose initial values are zero is continuous after adding preceding endpoint values; its distributional derivative is the piecewise derivative without jump terms.",grounds:[{location:"/inputs/3/statement",reason:"N007 supplies the smooth trajectory and stepwise smooth stages."},{location:"/definitions/27",reason:"D029 gives the integrals and affine increment."},{location:"/definitions/28",reason:"D030 gives the finite concatenation."}],justification:"The difference quotient of an integral differs from the integrand at t by at most its modulus of continuity on the shrinking interval. Substitution of the zero-start value makes adjacent endpoint formulas agree. Integrating by parts on the finitely many pieces cancels the internal boundary terms because the function is continuous."},
{id:"BG002",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",fact:"The projection P=I-P0 removes exactly the zero Fourier mode, preserves smoothness and contracts every Fourier Sobolev norm. For mean-zero f, ||f-Pw||_2<=||f-w||_2. Under L1 and gamma>0, ||f||_2<=M+1, its time-constant X norm equals ||f||_2, and 0<tau^gamma<=1 so 0<q_perp<=q_n<=2. Restricting the stated time supremum norms and X to a subinterval cannot increase them.",grounds:[{location:"/definitions/2",reason:"The Sobolev norms are nonnegative weighted Fourier sums."},{location:"/definitions/4",reason:"P0 is the constant zero Fourier coefficient."},{location:"/definitions/5",reason:"P=I-P0."},{location:"/definitions/18",reason:"X is the sum of the time L2 and derivative negative-Sobolev supremum norms."},{location:"/inputs/0/statement",reason:"gamma>0."},{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 gives mean-zero f."},{location:"/inference/argument/1/text",reason:"L1 states the actual comparison and timestep conditions; the projection and error identities are displayed here."}],justification:"Deleting a nonnegative summand decreases a convergent weighted sum, as follows by comparing finite partial sums and taking their suprema. Subtracting a spatial constant preserves smoothness, and P(f-w)=f-Pw when Pf=f. The H^gamma weights are at least one, so the L2 triangle inequality gives ||f||_2<=||w||_{H^gamma}+delta_n. The time derivative of a constant is zero. Suprema decrease on restriction. Finally tau^gamma=exp(gamma log tau) belongs to (0,1] for 0<tau<=1 and gamma>0."},
{id:"BG003",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/2/text",fact:"For any continuous L2-valued integrand G on [a,b], ||integral_a^b G(s) ds||_2<=integral_a^b ||G(s)||_2 ds<=(b-a)sup_{[a,b]}||G(s)||_2. Thus uniform J bounds in S003 integrate with a factor t-t_n, including the zero-length case.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 supplies smooth stages and their derivatives."},{location:"/inputs/2/statement",reason:"N006 supplies the J estimates and normal-form identities."},{location:"/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement",reason:"F002 supplies the uniform stage bounds under L1."}],justification:"The triangle inequality gives the integral inequality for Riemann sums. Continuity on a compact interval gives convergence of the sums in L2 and of the scalar norm sums, so the inequality passes to their limits. Bounding each scalar integrand by its supremum gives the length factor."},
{id:"BG004",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/3/text",fact:"For pstar=23/14>0 and W in L2, ||W||_{H^{-pstar}}<=||W||_2. Uniform a.e. bounds on the step derivative give the same L-infinity bound; grid endpoints have no effect on this essential supremum.",grounds:[{location:"/definitions/2",reason:"The Sobolev norms use weights <k>^{2s}, and time L-infinity denotes essential supremum."},{location:"/definitions/14",reason:"pstar=23/14>0."},{location:"/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement",reason:"F002 supplies the L2 bound for W_n and uniform stage estimates."},{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 identifies the a.e. residual derivative."}],justification:"Each weight <k>^{-2pstar} is at most one; compare the nonnegative Fourier sums and take square roots. The definition of essential supremum turns the uniform a.e. bound into the stated time norm. A finite set of grid times has measure zero."}
